[
{ch:7,q:"What is a covalent bond, according to the model used in this course?",choices:["The electrostatic attraction between a shared pair of electrons and the positively charged nuclei of the two bonded atoms","The complete transfer of one or more electrons from one atom to another","The attraction between a lattice of positive ions and a sea of delocalized electrons","The attraction between oppositely charged ions in a crystal lattice"],explain:"A covalent bond forms when a shared electron pair is simultaneously attracted to both bonded nuclei; electron transfer describes ionic bonding, and a sea of delocalized electrons describes metallic bonding."},
{ch:7,q:"According to the octet rule, atoms tend to gain a valence shell containing how many electrons, matching the electron arrangement of a noble gas?",choices:["Eight","Two","Six","Ten"],explain:"The octet rule describes atoms' tendency to acquire eight valence electrons (own plus shared), matching the stable arrangement of a noble gas; hydrogen is the main exception, filling its shell with only two electrons."},
{ch:7,q:"Why do noble gases form covalent bonds far less readily than other elements?",choices:["Apart from helium, their outer shells are already complete octets, leaving no driving force to share electrons","They have too few protons in their nuclei to attract any shared electrons","They have no electrons in their valence shell at all","Their atoms are too large to approach another atom closely enough to share electrons"],explain:"With a full outer shell already present (except helium, which has a full duet), noble gas atoms have no tendency to share electrons with another atom to complete an octet, making them very unreactive."},
{ch:7,q:"Why can two chlorine atoms form a covalent bond with each other, while a metal and a non-metal form an ionic bond instead?",choices:["Two chlorine atoms attract valence electrons with similar strength, so sharing (not complete transfer) is the only option, unlike the large electronegativity gap between a metal and a non-metal","Chlorine atoms contain no protons, so they cannot attract electrons at all","Ionic bonds can only form between atoms of the same element","Covalent bonds are always weaker than ionic bonds, regardless of the elements involved"],explain:"Atoms of similar electron-attracting power cannot pull an electron completely away from one another, so they share electrons instead; a large difference in electron-attracting power, as between a metal and non-metal, instead allows complete electron transfer and ionic bonding."},
{ch:7,q:"Which pair of elements are classic examples of central atoms that form an incomplete octet in stable molecules?",choices:["Beryllium and boron","Oxygen and nitrogen","Chlorine and fluorine","Carbon and silicon"],explain:"Beryllium and boron have too few valence electrons of their own to complete an octet through bonding, forming stable molecules such as BeCl<sub>2</sub> (4 electrons around Be) and BF<sub>3</sub> (6 electrons around B)."},
{ch:7,q:"Besides hydrogen and the elements that form an incomplete octet, which other groups of species are recognized exceptions to the octet rule?",choices:["Expanded-octet species, some transition elements, and free radicals with an unpaired electron","Only noble gas compounds","Only diatomic molecules of the same element","Only ionic compounds"],explain:"Beyond hydrogen's duet and incomplete octets on small atoms like Be and B, the octet rule also has exceptions for expanded-octet species (period 3 and below), certain transition elements, and free radicals, which by definition have an odd, unpaired electron."},
{ch:7,q:"How many valence electrons must be shown in total when drawing the Lewis formula of CCl<sub>4</sub>?",choices:["32","8","28","36"],explain:"Carbon contributes 4 valence electrons and each of the four chlorine atoms contributes 7, giving 4 + (7 &times; 4) = 32 valence electrons in total."},
{ch:7,q:"When drawing the Lewis formula of an ion such as OH<sup>&minus;</sup>, how should the total valence electron count be adjusted compared with a neutral molecule?",choices:["Add one electron for each unit of negative charge, or subtract one electron for each unit of positive charge","Always subtract exactly two electrons, regardless of the ion's charge","Ignore the charge completely when counting valence electrons","Double the valence electron count for every negative charge present"],explain:"For an ion, the usual valence electron sum is adjusted by adding one electron per unit of negative charge or subtracting one electron per unit of positive charge, since the ion has gained or lost electrons relative to the neutral atoms."},
{ch:7,q:"In the Lewis formula of the hydroxide ion, OH<sup>&minus;</sup>, how many lone pairs are present on the oxygen atom?",choices:["Three","Two","One","Four"],explain:"OH<sup>&minus;</sup> has 6 (O) + 1 (H) + 1 (for the negative charge) = 8 valence electrons; one bonding pair is used for the O&ndash;H bond, leaving 6 electrons as three lone pairs on oxygen, giving oxygen a complete octet."},
{ch:7,q:"Why do beryllium and boron form stable molecules, such as BeCl<sub>2</sub> and BF<sub>3</sub>, with an incomplete octet on the central atom?",choices:["They are too small, with too few valence electrons of their own, to form enough bonds to complete a full octet","They have more than eight valence electrons already, so an octet would be an overcrowded structure","They cannot form covalent bonds under any circumstances","Their compounds always exist as ions, not covalent molecules"],explain:"Beryllium (2 valence electrons) and boron (3 valence electrons) are simply too small and electron-poor to reach a full octet of 8 electrons through ordinary single bonding, resulting in stable molecules with only 4 or 6 electrons around the central atom."},
{ch:7,q:"Which total number of valence electrons is correctly calculated for the molecule BF<sub>3</sub>?",choices:["24, from 3 (B) + (7 &times; 3) (three F atoms)","32, from 3 (B) + (7 &times; 4) (four F atoms)","16, from 3 (B) + (7 &times; 1) (one F atom only)","21, from 3 (B) + (6 &times; 3) (three O atoms, in error)"],explain:"Boron contributes 3 valence electrons and each of the three fluorine atoms contributes 7, giving 3 + (7 &times; 3) = 24 valence electrons in total for BF<sub>3</sub>."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does the Lewis formula of O<sub>2</sub> require a double bond rather than a single bond between the two oxygen atoms?",choices:["A single bond alone would leave each oxygen with only 6 electrons, short of a full octet, so a lone pair from each oxygen must become a second shared pair","Oxygen atoms cannot form single bonds under any circumstances","A double bond uses fewer of the 12 available valence electrons than a single bond would","Double bonds only form between different elements, never between two atoms of the same element"],explain:"With only a single bond, each oxygen would have just 6 electrons; converting one lone pair per oxygen into a second shared pair (a double bond) gives each oxygen a full octet of 8 electrons, using all 12 valence electrons."},
{ch:7,q:"For bonds between the same two elements, what happens to bond length and bond strength as the number of shared electron pairs increases from a single to a triple bond?",choices:["Bond length decreases and bond strength (bond enthalpy) increases","Bond length increases and bond strength decreases","Both bond length and bond strength increase","Both bond length and bond strength stay the same"],explain:"More shared electron pairs pull the bonded nuclei closer together with a stronger electrostatic attraction, so triple bonds are shorter and stronger than double bonds, which are shorter and stronger than single bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"Why is a C=C double bond not exactly twice as strong as a C&ndash;C single bond?",choices:["A double bond is made of one strong sigma bond and one weaker pi bond, rather than two identical bonds","Double bonds always contain fewer total electrons than single bonds","Carbon atoms repel each other more strongly in a double bond than in a single bond","Double bonds are actually weaker than single bonds in every case"],explain:"A double bond consists of one sigma bond (strong, formed by head-on orbital overlap) and one pi bond (weaker, formed by sideways overlap), so the overall bond strength is less than simply doubling the single-bond strength."},
{ch:7,q:"Moving down group 17 from Cl&ndash;Cl to Br&ndash;Br to I&ndash;I, halogen&ndash;halogen bonds generally become longer and weaker as atomic radius increases. Which halogen is a notable outlier to this trend?",choices:["Fluorine, F<sub>2</sub>, because of unusually strong repulsion between its lone pairs at such a small atomic size","Chlorine, Cl<sub>2</sub>","Bromine, Br<sub>2</sub>","Iodine, I<sub>2</sub>"],explain:"F<sub>2</sub> is a notable outlier: although fluorine is the smallest halogen, its bond is weaker than expected because its lone pairs are forced very close together, causing unusually strong repulsion."},
{ch:7,q:"What is a coordination (dative covalent) bond?",choices:["A covalent bond in which both electrons of the shared pair originate from the same atom","A covalent bond in which one electron comes from each bonded atom","An ionic bond formed by complete electron transfer","A bond that is weaker than a normal covalent bond of the same type"],explain:"In a coordination bond, the donor atom contributes both electrons of the shared pair, unlike an ordinary covalent bond where each atom contributes one electron; once formed, the bond is otherwise identical to a normal covalent bond."},
{ch:7,q:"In the formation of the ammonium ion, NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>, from NH<sub>3</sub> and H<sup>+</sup>, which statement is correct?",choices:["Nitrogen donates its lone pair to the incoming H<sup>+</sup> ion, forming a coordination bond that becomes indistinguishable from the other three N&ndash;H bonds","The H<sup>+</sup> ion donates an electron pair to nitrogen","All four N&ndash;H bonds in NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> remain chemically distinguishable from one another","No new bond is formed; NH<sub>3</sub> and H<sup>+</sup> simply associate through hydrogen bonding"],explain:"H<sup>+</sup> has no electrons of its own, so nitrogen's lone pair supplies both electrons of the new bond; once formed, this coordination bond is identical in every way to the other three N&ndash;H bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"In carbon monoxide, CO, which statement correctly describes the triple bond?",choices:["Two of the three shared pairs form in the usual way, while the third is a coordination bond with both electrons donated by oxygen","All three shared pairs are coordination bonds donated entirely by carbon","CO does not actually contain a triple bond","The coordination bond in CO is weaker than the other two bonds in the triple bond"],explain:"In CO, carbon alone could not complete its octet with its own 4 valence electrons; oxygen donates both electrons of the third shared pair as a coordination bond, and all three bonds in the finished triple bond are identical in length and strength."},
{ch:7,q:"Why can ammonia, NH<sub>3</sub>, react directly with boron trifluoride, BF<sub>3</sub>, to form H<sub>3</sub>N&rarr;BF<sub>3</sub>?",choices:["Nitrogen's lone pair can be donated into boron's empty valence space, forming a coordination bond that gives both atoms a complete octet","Boron and nitrogen simply swap one electron each in an ordinary covalent bond","BF<sub>3</sub> already has a complete octet and cannot accept any further electrons","Ammonia has no lone pair available to donate"],explain:"BF<sub>3</sub> has only an incomplete octet of 6 electrons around boron; ammonia's nitrogen lone pair fills this gap by forming a coordination bond, giving boron a complete octet in the product."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does gaseous aluminium chloride exist as the dimer Al<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>6</sub> rather than as separate AlCl<sub>3</sub> molecules?",choices:["Two chlorine atoms each donate a lone pair to the aluminium of the neighbouring molecule, forming coordination bonds that give every atom a complete octet","Aluminium and chlorine form only ionic bonds in this compound","AlCl<sub>3</sub> molecules repel each other too strongly to exist separately","Chlorine atoms cannot form more than one bond under any circumstances"],explain:"Each AlCl<sub>3</sub> unit has an incomplete octet on aluminium; bridging coordination bonds, in which chlorine atoms from each unit donate a lone pair to the aluminium of the other unit, give every atom in the dimer Al<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>6</sub> a complete octet."},
{ch:7,q:"In the VSEPR model, what is meant by an 'electron domain'?",choices:["Any region of electron density around a central atom, including a single bond, a double bond, a triple bond, or a lone pair","Only a non-bonding (lone) pair of electrons","Only a single covalent bond, never a multiple bond","The total number of protons in the nucleus of the central atom"],explain:"An electron domain is any region of electron density around the central atom; a double or triple bond counts as only one domain because all its bonding pairs point in the same direction."},
{ch:7,q:"How many electron domains surround the central oxygen atom in ozone, O<sub>3</sub>, given its Lewis formula of one O&ndash;O single bond, one O=O double bond, and one lone pair?",choices:["Three","Two","Four","Five"],explain:"Counting the single bond, the double bond (as one domain, regardless of its two shared pairs), and the lone pair gives a total of three electron domains around the central oxygen in ozone."},
{ch:7,q:"According to the VSEPR model, which ordering of repulsive strength between different types of electron domain is correct?",choices:["Non-bonding (lone) pair &gt; multiple bond &gt; single bond","Single bond &gt; multiple bond &gt; non-bonding pair","Multiple bond &gt; non-bonding pair &gt; single bond","All electron domains repel with exactly equal strength"],explain:"Lone pairs cause the most repulsion because their electron density is not shared between two nuclei, and multiple bonds cause more repulsion than single bonds because they contain extra electron pairs, giving the order lone pair &gt; multiple bond &gt; single bond."},
{ch:7,q:"What is the electron domain geometry and bond angle of a species with exactly two electron domains around its central atom, such as CO<sub>2</sub> or BeCl<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["Linear, 180&deg;","Trigonal planar, 120&deg;","Tetrahedral, 109.5&deg;","Bent, 104.5&deg;"],explain:"With only two electron domains, repulsion is minimized by placing them at exactly 180&deg; to each other, giving a linear arrangement, as seen in CO<sub>2</sub> and BeCl<sub>2</sub>."},
{ch:7,q:"BH<sub>3</sub> and AlCl<sub>3</sub> both have three electron domains, all of which are bonding pairs, around their central atom. What is the resulting molecular geometry and bond angle?",choices:["Trigonal planar, 120&deg;","Tetrahedral, 109.5&deg;","Bent, approximately 117&deg;","Linear, 180&deg;"],explain:"With three bonding electron domains and no lone pairs, repulsion is minimized by spreading the domains out at 120&deg; in a single flat plane, giving a trigonal planar shape."},
{ch:7,q:"Ozone, O<sub>3</sub>, has three electron domains around its central oxygen. What is its molecular shape and approximate bond angle?",choices:["Bent (V-shaped), approximately 117&deg;, less than the ideal 120&deg;","Trigonal planar, exactly 120&deg;","Linear, 180&deg;","Tetrahedral, 109.5&deg;"],explain:"Because one of the three electron domains is a lone pair rather than an atom, the molecular shape traced by the atoms is bent, and the extra repulsion from the lone pair and double bond compresses the O&ndash;O&ndash;O angle to about 117&deg;."},
{ch:7,q:"In methanal, HCOH, why is the H&ndash;C&ndash;H angle slightly less than 120&deg; while the O=C&ndash;H angle is slightly more than 120&deg;?",choices:["The C=O double bond causes slightly more repulsion than the single C&ndash;H bonds, pushing the two C&ndash;H bonds closer together","Oxygen has a higher atomic mass than hydrogen, which distorts the angles","Methanal actually has four electron domains, not three, around carbon","Hydrogen atoms repel each other more strongly than any other pair of atoms in the molecule"],explain:"Because a double bond causes more repulsion than a single bond, the C=O domain pushes the two C&ndash;H bonds slightly closer together, closing the H&ndash;C&ndash;H angle below 120&deg; while opening the O=C&ndash;H angle slightly above it."},
{ch:7,q:"CH<sub>4</sub> has four bonding electron domains and no lone pairs around its central carbon atom. What is its molecular geometry and bond angle?",choices:["Tetrahedral, 109.5&deg;","Trigonal pyramidal, 107&deg;","Bent, 104.5&deg;","Trigonal planar, 120&deg;"],explain:"With four bonding domains and no lone pairs, the domains point towards the four corners of a regular tetrahedron, each separated by 109.5&deg;, giving a tetrahedral molecular shape identical to the electron domain geometry."},
{ch:7,q:"NH<sub>3</sub> has four electron domains (3 bonding, 1 lone pair) around nitrogen. What is its molecular geometry and approximate H&ndash;N&ndash;H bond angle?",choices:["Trigonal pyramidal, approximately 107&deg;","Tetrahedral, 109.5&deg;","Bent, approximately 104.5&deg;","Trigonal planar, 120&deg;"],explain:"Looking only at the three bonded hydrogen atoms, NH<sub>3</sub> is trigonal pyramidal, with the single lone pair compressing the bond angle from 109.5&deg; down to about 107&deg;."},
{ch:7,q:"H<sub>2</sub>O has four electron domains (2 bonding, 2 lone pairs) around oxygen. What is its molecular geometry and approximate H&ndash;O&ndash;H bond angle?",choices:["Bent (V-shaped), approximately 104.5&deg;","Trigonal pyramidal, approximately 107&deg;","Tetrahedral, 109.5&deg;","Linear, 180&deg;"],explain:"Two lone pairs push harder on the remaining two bonding domains than a single lone pair does in NH<sub>3</sub>, compressing the H&ndash;O&ndash;H angle further, from 109.5&deg; down to about 104.5&deg;, giving a bent molecular shape."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does increasing the number of lone pairs on a tetrahedral central atom cause progressively smaller bond angles, as seen going from CH<sub>4</sub> (109.5&deg;) to NH<sub>3</sub> (107&deg;) to H<sub>2</sub>O (104.5&deg;)?",choices:["Each additional lone pair adds extra repulsion that pushes the remaining bonding domains closer together","Lone pairs have no effect on bond angles; the changes are caused only by differing atomic masses","Adding lone pairs increases the total number of electron domains from four to five or six","Water and ammonia do not actually have four electron domains around their central atom"],explain:"Lone pairs cause more repulsion than bonding pairs; as more lone pairs are added to a fixed tetrahedral electron domain geometry, they compress the remaining bonding domains into a progressively smaller angle."},
{ch:7,q:"What does electronegativity measure?",choices:["The ability of an atom to attract a shared pair of electrons in a covalent bond","The number of valence electrons an atom possesses","The total number of protons in an atom's nucleus","The physical size (radius) of an atom"],explain:"Electronegativity is a measure of an atom's ability to attract the shared electron pair in a covalent bond, expressed on the dimensionless Pauling scale from about 0.7 to 4.0."},
{ch:7,q:"Given EN(H) = 2.2 and EN(Cl) = 3.2, how should the H&ndash;Cl bond be classified, using the resulting &Delta;EN of 1.0?",choices:["Polar covalent","Non-polar (pure) covalent","Ionic","Metallic"],explain:"&Delta;EN = |3.2 &minus; 2.2| = 1.0, which falls within the roughly 0.5&ndash;1.8 range conventionally classified as polar covalent."},
{ch:7,q:"Using EN(Na) = 0.9 and EN(Cl) = 3.2, how should the Na&ndash;Cl bond be classified?",choices:["Ionic, since &Delta;EN = 2.3 is above the roughly 1.8 threshold","Polar covalent, since &Delta;EN = 2.3 falls below 1.8","Non-polar covalent, since the two elements are from the same period","Metallic, since sodium is a metal"],explain:"&Delta;EN = |3.2 &minus; 0.9| = 2.3, above the roughly 1.8 threshold used to distinguish polar covalent from ionic bonding, so the Na&ndash;Cl bond is classified as ionic."},
{ch:7,q:"Among the hydrogen halides, which shows the correct order of decreasing bond polarity?",choices:["H&ndash;F &gt; H&ndash;Cl &gt; H&ndash;Br &gt; H&ndash;I","H&ndash;I &gt; H&ndash;Br &gt; H&ndash;Cl &gt; H&ndash;F","H&ndash;Cl &gt; H&ndash;F &gt; H&ndash;I &gt; H&ndash;Br","All four hydrogen halide bonds are equally polar"],explain:"Electronegativity decreases going down group 17 from F to I, so the electronegativity difference (and hence bond polarity) with hydrogen decreases in the order H&ndash;F &gt; H&ndash;Cl &gt; H&ndash;Br &gt; H&ndash;I."},
{ch:7,q:"Why is the C&ndash;H bond, so common throughout organic chemistry, usually treated as essentially non-polar even though a small electronegativity difference exists?",choices:["Carbon (EN 2.6) is only very slightly more electronegative than hydrogen (EN 2.2), giving a very small &Delta;EN","Carbon and hydrogen have exactly identical electronegativity values","Hydrogen is actually more electronegative than carbon in every bond","The C&ndash;H bond is a purely ionic bond, not a covalent one"],explain:"Carbon's electronegativity (2.6) is only marginally higher than hydrogen's (2.2), giving a small &Delta;EN of about 0.4, which is low enough that the C&ndash;H bond is usually treated as essentially non-polar in practice."},
{ch:7,q:"Which bonds are truly non-polar, with an electronegativity difference of exactly zero?",choices:["Bonds between two atoms of the same element, such as in F<sub>2</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub>, and Cl<sub>2</sub>","Bonds between any two non-metal atoms, regardless of identity","Bonds between a metal and a non-metal","All single covalent bonds, regardless of the atoms involved"],explain:"Only bonds between two atoms of the same element have a zero electronegativity difference and are truly non-polar (sometimes called pure covalent); every other covalent bond has at least some degree of polarity."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does hydrogen chloride, HCl, conduct electricity when dissolved in water, even though it is a covalent molecular substance?",choices:["HCl ionizes completely in water, producing mobile H<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> ions that carry charge, reflecting its position on the covalent&ndash;ionic bonding continuum","All covalent substances conduct electricity once dissolved in any solvent","HCl gas conducts electricity even without water present","Water itself becomes ionic once HCl is added to it"],explain:"HCl's bond is polar enough that, in water, it ionizes completely to give mobile H<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> ions, illustrating how a sufficiently polar covalent bond can show properties that overlap with ionic behaviour."},
{ch:7,q:"CO<sub>2</sub> contains two polar C=O bonds, yet the molecule as a whole is non-polar. Why?",choices:["The molecule is linear, so the two equal and opposite bond dipoles point in exactly opposite directions and cancel out","Carbon and oxygen have identical electronegativities, so neither C=O bond is actually polar","CO<sub>2</sub> molecules have no electrons at all in their bonds","Only single bonds can be polar, and CO<sub>2</sub> contains double bonds"],explain:"Because CO<sub>2</sub> is linear (180&deg;) and both C=O bond dipoles are identical in size, they point directly against each other and cancel exactly by vector addition, giving a net dipole of zero despite the individually polar bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"Why is chloromethane, CH<sub>3</sub>Cl, a polar molecule?",choices:["Its single, strongly polar C&ndash;Cl bond dipole is not cancelled by any equal and opposite dipole elsewhere on the tetrahedral carbon","All four bonds around carbon are identical, so their dipoles cancel completely","Chlorine and carbon have identical electronegativities","The molecule is perfectly symmetrical, like CCl<sub>4</sub>"],explain:"The three C&ndash;H bonds are only very weakly polar, so they cannot cancel out the much stronger C&ndash;Cl bond dipole, leaving chloromethane with a clear net dipole pointing towards the chlorine atom."},
{ch:7,q:"Between cis- and trans-1,2-dichloroethene, which isomer is polar, and why?",choices:["The cis isomer, because its two identical C&ndash;Cl bond dipoles point in similar directions on the same side and reinforce rather than cancel","The trans isomer, because its two C&ndash;Cl bond dipoles point in similar directions","Neither isomer is polar, since both contain the same bonds","Both isomers are equally polar, since molecular geometry does not affect polarity"],explain:"In the cis isomer both chlorine atoms sit on the same side of the C=C double bond, so their equal bond dipoles reinforce each other, giving a net dipole; in the trans isomer the dipoles point in nearly opposite directions and largely cancel, making it non-polar or only weakly polar."},
{ch:7,q:"Why are BF<sub>3</sub> and CCl<sub>4</sub> both non-polar molecules, despite containing individually polar bonds?",choices:["All the bonds attached to the central atom are identical and symmetrically arranged, so their bond dipoles cancel exactly","Boron and carbon have no electronegativity at all","Fluorine and chlorine atoms do not form polar bonds with boron or carbon","Both molecules contain only non-bonding pairs of electrons"],explain:"BF<sub>3</sub>'s three identical B&ndash;F dipoles (120&deg; apart) and CCl<sub>4</sub>'s four identical C&ndash;Cl dipoles (tetrahedral) are each arranged with perfect symmetry around the central atom, so by vector addition their dipoles cancel exactly, leaving a non-polar molecule overall."},
{ch:7,q:"What is a covalent network (giant covalent/macromolecular) structure?",choices:["A crystalline lattice in which every atom is linked to its neighbours by covalent bonds repeating indefinitely, so the whole crystal behaves as one enormous molecule","A small, discrete molecule with a fixed, finite number of atoms","A lattice of positive ions held together by delocalized electrons","A regular arrangement of oppositely charged ions"],explain:"Unlike most covalent substances, which form small discrete molecules, a covalent network structure is a lattice where covalent bonding repeats indefinitely throughout the crystal, giving it no fixed molecular size."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does graphite conduct electricity well along its layers, while diamond does not conduct at all?",choices:["Each carbon in graphite is bonded to only 3 neighbours, leaving one delocalized electron per atom free to move within a layer, whereas every one of diamond's 4 valence electrons per carbon is locked into a localized bond","Graphite contains metal atoms, but diamond does not","Diamond's carbon atoms carry an overall positive charge","Graphite has stronger covalent bonds than diamond"],explain:"In graphite, each carbon forms only 3 covalent bonds, leaving one electron per atom delocalized and mobile within each hexagonal layer; in diamond, all 4 valence electrons per carbon are used in localized tetrahedral bonds, leaving none free to conduct."},
{ch:7,q:"Which allotrope of carbon consists of a single, isolated one-atom-thick layer of graphite's hexagonal sheet?",choices:["Graphene","Diamond","C<sub>60</sub> fullerene","Silicon dioxide"],explain:"Graphene is a single isolated hexagonal carbon sheet only one atom thick, first isolated in 2004; graphite is many such sheets stacked together, and C<sub>60</sub> fullerene is a closed spherical molecule rather than an extended sheet."},
{ch:7,q:"Why is graphite soft and slippery, even though its individual covalent bonds are very strong?",choices:["The separate hexagonal layers are held to each other only by weak London dispersion forces, so the layers can slide easily over one another","All of graphite's bonds, including those between layers, are weak intermolecular forces","Graphite contains no covalent bonds at all","Graphite's carbon atoms are not arranged in a regular pattern"],explain:"Within each layer, carbon atoms are joined by strong covalent bonds in a hexagonal pattern, but the separate layers are held together only by weak London dispersion forces, allowing them to slide over each other easily, giving graphite its softness and lubricating properties."},
{ch:7,q:"Which statement correctly describes the structure of silicon dioxide, SiO<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["A giant covalent lattice in which each silicon atom is bonded to four oxygen atoms and each oxygen bridges two silicon atoms, with SiO<sub>2</sub> only an empirical ratio, not a discrete molecule","A small discrete molecule containing one silicon atom double-bonded to two separate oxygen atoms, analogous to CO<sub>2</sub>","An ionic lattice of Si<sup>4+</sup> and O<sup>2&minus;</sup> ions","A metallic lattice of silicon cations and delocalized electrons"],explain:"SiO<sub>2</sub> forms a giant covalent network in which each Si is bonded tetrahedrally to 4 oxygens and each oxygen bridges two silicons; the formula SiO<sub>2</sub> only expresses the 1:2 atom ratio, since the true structure is one continuous lattice, unlike the small discrete CO<sub>2</sub> molecule."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does carbon readily form long chains and rings with itself (catenation), while silicon is instead found mainly bonded to oxygen in silicate minerals?",choices:["Carbon's smaller atomic radius gives the C&ndash;C bond a much higher bond enthalpy than the Si&ndash;Si bond, while silicon forms an especially strong bond with oxygen instead","Silicon has more valence electrons than carbon","Carbon cannot form bonds to oxygen at all","Silicon atoms repel each other electrostatically"],explain:"Carbon's small radius gives a strong, stable C&ndash;C bond (about 346 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) that supports catenation, whereas the weaker Si&ndash;Si bond (about 226 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) and especially strong Si&ndash;O bond (about 466 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) mean silicon is found mainly bonded to oxygen in nature."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does the electrical conductivity of pure silicon increase sharply as its temperature rises?",choices:["Thermal energy frees some electrons from the covalent lattice, leaving mobile positive holes that allow other electrons to move through the structure","Silicon melts at a very low temperature, releasing free ions","Silicon atoms gain additional valence electrons as temperature increases","Silicon becomes ionic at higher temperatures"],explain:"As temperature rises, thermal energy provides enough energy to free some electrons from silicon's covalent bonds; each freed electron leaves behind a positively charged hole, allowing other electrons to move through the lattice and increasing conductivity."},
{ch:7,q:"What is the effect of 'doping' silicon with small amounts of elements such as arsenic or boron?",choices:["It increases silicon's electrical conductivity, forming the basis of the modern semiconductor electronics industry","It converts silicon into an ionic compound","It destroys all of silicon's covalent bonding entirely","It has no measurable effect on silicon's properties"],explain:"Doping introduces trace impurity atoms into the silicon lattice that enhance its electrical conductivity, a process that underlies the semiconductor technology used throughout the modern electronics industry."},
{ch:7,q:"Which ordering correctly ranks intermolecular forces from weakest to strongest, for molecules of comparable molar mass?",choices:["London (dispersion) forces &lt; dipole&ndash;dipole forces &lt; hydrogen bonding","Hydrogen bonding &lt; dipole&ndash;dipole forces &lt; London (dispersion) forces","Dipole&ndash;dipole forces &lt; London (dispersion) forces &lt; hydrogen bonding","All intermolecular forces are of exactly equal strength"],explain:"For molecules of similar molar mass, London dispersion forces are weakest, dipole&ndash;dipole forces are intermediate, and hydrogen bonding is strongest; all are still much weaker than covalent or ionic bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"What causes a London (dispersion) force to arise between two non-polar molecules such as Cl<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["A constantly shifting electron cloud creates a brief instantaneous dipole in one molecule, which induces a matching dipole in a neighbouring molecule","A permanent, fixed separation of charge exists in every non-polar molecule","Non-polar molecules exchange electrons completely with each other","Non-polar molecules carry a permanent net electrical charge"],explain:"Random, momentary fluctuations in electron density create a temporary (instantaneous) dipole, which induces a corresponding dipole in a neighbouring molecule; the resulting brief attraction is a London dispersion force, present between every pair of molecules."},
{ch:7,q:"Down group 17, boiling points increase from F<sub>2</sub> (&minus;188&deg;C) to Cl<sub>2</sub> (&minus;34&deg;C) to Br<sub>2</sub> (59&deg;C) to I<sub>2</sub> (185&deg;C). What does this trend demonstrate?",choices:["London dispersion forces strengthen as molecules become larger and gain more electrons","Hydrogen bonding becomes stronger down the group","Dipole&ndash;dipole forces become weaker down the group","Covalent bond strength within each diatomic molecule decreases down the group"],explain:"All the halogens are non-polar, so only London dispersion forces act between their molecules; as molar mass and electron number increase down the group, the larger, more easily distorted electron clouds create stronger dispersion forces, raising the boiling point."},
{ch:7,q:"Propane (M = 44 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, non-polar) boils at 229 K, while methoxymethane (M = 46 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, polar) boils at the higher temperature of 249 K. What best explains this difference?",choices:["Methoxymethane experiences additional dipole&ndash;dipole attractions on top of London forces, while propane has only London forces, despite their very similar molar masses","Methoxymethane has much stronger covalent bonds than propane","Propane has a much larger molar mass than methoxymethane","Methoxymethane can form hydrogen bonds between its own molecules"],explain:"Since the molar masses are almost identical, London forces are similar in strength for both; the higher boiling point of methoxymethane is explained by its permanent dipole, which adds dipole&ndash;dipole attraction on top of London forces (methoxymethane has no O&ndash;H bond, so it cannot hydrogen bond with itself)."},
{ch:7,q:"What is the 'van der Waals' forces' umbrella term generally understood to include?",choices:["London dispersion forces, dipole&ndash;induced dipole forces, and dipole&ndash;dipole forces, but not hydrogen bonding or ionic/covalent bonds","Only hydrogen bonds between water molecules","Only the forces found inside covalent network structures","Every type of chemical bond, including ionic and covalent bonds"],explain:"Van der Waals' forces is an inclusive term covering London dispersion, dipole&ndash;induced dipole, and dipole&ndash;dipole attractions &mdash; any intermolecular attraction that does not involve full ionic charges or the formation of an actual covalent or hydrogen bond."},
{ch:7,q:"A hydrogen bond can only form when a hydrogen atom is covalently bonded directly to which elements?",choices:["Fluorine, oxygen, or nitrogen","Any non-metal element","Carbon or silicon","Chlorine or bromine"],explain:"Hydrogen bonding requires hydrogen to be covalently bonded to one of only three small, highly electronegative elements &mdash; F, O, or N &mdash; leaving hydrogen with an unusually concentrated, exposed partial positive charge able to interact strongly with a lone pair on a neighbouring F, O, or N atom."},
{ch:7,q:"Ethanol (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH, boiling point +79&deg;C) and methoxymethane (CH<sub>3</sub>OCH<sub>3</sub>, boiling point &minus;23&deg;C) are isomers with the identical molar mass of 46 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What best explains ethanol's much higher boiling point?",choices:["Ethanol has an O&ndash;H bond and can hydrogen bond between its own molecules, while methoxymethane's hydrogens are all bonded to carbon and cannot hydrogen bond","Ethanol has a much larger molar mass than methoxymethane","Methoxymethane has stronger covalent bonds than ethanol","Ethanol molecules are non-polar while methoxymethane molecules are polar"],explain:"Despite identical molar mass, only ethanol has an O&ndash;H bond, allowing hydrogen bonding between its molecules; methoxymethane's hydrogens are bonded only to carbon, so it can only form weaker dipole&ndash;dipole and London forces, giving it a much lower boiling point."},
{ch:7,q:"Why is solid ice less dense than liquid water, an unusual property for a solid?",choices:["In ice, every water molecule is locked into the maximum of four hydrogen bonds, forming a rigid, open lattice with empty space, less densely packed than the liquid","Ice molecules are held together by weaker forces than liquid water molecules","Ice contains fewer hydrogen atoms per molecule than liquid water","Water expands because its covalent O&ndash;H bonds break upon freezing"],explain:"In ice, each water molecule forms the maximum four hydrogen bonds to neighbours, creating a rigid, open tetrahedral lattice with empty space at its centre; this open structure is less densely packed than liquid water, where fewer hydrogen bonds exist on average, making ice float."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does diamond have an extremely high melting point (around 4000&deg;C), unlike most covalent molecular substances?",choices:["Melting diamond requires breaking strong covalent bonds throughout its giant lattice, not just weak intermolecular forces between separate molecules","Diamond molecules are held together only by weak London dispersion forces","Diamond is actually an ionic compound","Diamond has no covalent bonds at all"],explain:"Diamond is a covalent network structure, so melting it means breaking the strong covalent bonds that extend throughout the entire lattice, requiring vastly more energy than overcoming the weak intermolecular forces broken when a molecular covalent substance melts."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does sugar (a polar solute) fail to dissolve well in cooking oil (a non-polar solvent)?",choices:["Neither substance can interact effectively with the other's intermolecular forces, since 'like dissolves like'","Sugar and oil react chemically to form a precipitate","Cooking oil is an ionic compound","Sugar has no intermolecular forces of any kind"],explain:"The guiding solubility principle is 'like dissolves like': polar solutes dissolve well in polar solvents (through dipole&ndash;dipole/hydrogen bonding) and non-polar solutes dissolve well in non-polar solvents (through London forces), but polar and non-polar substances generally do not dissolve well in each other."},
{ch:7,q:"In chromatography, what determines how quickly a component of a mixture moves relative to the solvent front?",choices:["Its relative attraction, through intermolecular forces, to the stationary phase versus the mobile phase","Its molar mass alone, regardless of intermolecular forces","Its colour, which is unrelated to intermolecular forces","The temperature of the surrounding room only"],explain:"Components with a stronger attraction to the stationary phase move more slowly, while those more strongly attracted to the moving mobile phase travel further and faster; this difference in relative attraction is what separates the mixture."},
{ch:7,q:"In paper chromatography, what actually acts as the stationary phase?",choices:["Water held in place by hydrogen bonds to the &ndash;OH groups of the paper's cellulose fibres","The dry cellulose fibres of the paper themselves, with no water involved","The solvent used to develop the chromatogram","Silica or alumina coated onto the paper"],explain:"Chromatography paper contains roughly 10% water held by hydrogen bonds to the cellulose's &ndash;OH groups; this trapped water, not the dry paper itself, is the true stationary phase in paper chromatography."},
{ch:7,q:"On a paper chromatogram, the origin-to-solvent-front distance is 8.0 cm, and a spot has travelled 6.0 cm from the origin. What is the R<sub>f</sub> value of this spot?",choices:["0.75","1.33","6.0","2.0"],explain:"R<sub>f</sub> = distance moved by the component &divide; distance moved by the solvent = 6.0 cm &divide; 8.0 cm = 0.75."},
{ch:7,q:"What is the stationary phase typically used in thin layer chromatography (TLC)?",choices:["A thin, uniform layer of silica or alumina coated onto a rigid backing","Chromatography paper soaked in water","A pool of liquid solvent at the bottom of a tank","A metal wire mesh"],explain:"TLC uses a thin layer of silica (silicon dioxide) or alumina (aluminium oxide) coated onto glass, metal, or plastic; both surfaces contain polar &ndash;OH groups that can hydrogen bond with sample components, just as the water in paper does."},
{ch:7,q:"Why can the same compound give very different R<sub>f</sub> values when analysed with two different solvents?",choices:["Changing the solvent changes the balance of intermolecular attractions for every component, altering how far each travels relative to the solvent front","R<sub>f</sub> values are fixed constants that never change for a given compound","The stationary phase chemically reacts with the solvent to change the compound's structure","R<sub>f</sub> values only depend on the compound's molar mass, not on the solvent used"],explain:"An R<sub>f</sub> value is only meaningful when compared under matching conditions, because a different solvent changes the relative attraction of each component to the mobile versus stationary phase, which can give the same compound a very different R<sub>f</sub> value."},
{ch:7,q:"What does it mean for a species to show 'resonance' in its Lewis structure?",choices:["More than one valid Lewis formula can be drawn for the same arrangement of atoms, differing only in the position of a double bond, with the true structure being a hybrid of all of them","The molecule physically flips back and forth between two different structures over time","The species contains no double bonds at all","The species must contain an expanded octet on its central atom"],explain:"Resonance structures are different valid Lewis formulas for the same skeleton of atoms, differing only in double bond placement; the true structure is a single, fixed hybrid of all the contributing structures, not an alternation between them."},
{ch:7,q:"Experimental data show that ozone, O<sub>3</sub>, has two identical oxygen&ndash;oxygen bonds, each intermediate in length and strength between a single and a double bond. What explains this observation?",choices:["Ozone's two valid Lewis structures (with the double bond on either side) are not two separate forms but combine into a single resonance hybrid with delocalized electrons","Ozone actually contains three single bonds and no double bonds at all","The oxygen atoms in ozone constantly swap positions with each other","Ozone's Lewis structure cannot be drawn using the normal rules at all"],explain:"Because the double bond in ozone's Lewis formula could equally be placed on either O&ndash;O bond, the true structure is a resonance hybrid with the double-bond character shared equally between both bonds, giving two identical, intermediate bonds rather than one true single and one true double bond."},
{ch:7,q:"How many equivalent resonance structures are needed to represent the delocalized bonding in the carbonate ion, CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>?",choices:["Three, since the double bond could equally be placed on any one of the three oxygen atoms","One, since carbonate has only one possible Lewis structure","Two, matching the two possible positions of a lone pair","Four, one for each atom in the ion"],explain:"Carbonate's Lewis formula has one C=O double bond and two C&ndash;O single bonds; since the double bond could equally be drawn to any of the three identical oxygen atoms, three equivalent resonance structures exist, giving a hybrid with three identical, intermediate C&ndash;O bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does methanoic acid, HCOOH, have one distinct C=O bond and one distinct C&ndash;O bond, while the methanoate ion, HCOO<sup>&minus;</sup>, has two identical, intermediate carbon&ndash;oxygen bonds?",choices:["Methanoic acid has only one possible Lewis structure, while methanoate has two equivalent resonance structures with the double bond placed on either oxygen","Methanoic acid and methanoate have identical bonding in every respect","Methanoate contains no oxygen atoms bonded to carbon","Methanoic acid has more valence electrons than methanoate"],explain:"Removing the acidic hydrogen from HCOOH to form HCOO<sup>&minus;</sup> creates two identical oxygen atoms, either of which could bear the double bond, giving two equivalent resonance structures and a hybrid with two identical, intermediate C&ndash;O bonds, unlike the single fixed structure of the parent acid."},
{ch:7,q:"Why is molecular oxygen, O<sub>2</sub>, dissociated only by shorter-wavelength (higher energy) UV light than ozone, O<sub>3</sub>?",choices:["O<sub>2</sub>'s full, undelocalized double bond is stronger on average than ozone's delocalized, intermediate-strength O&ndash;O bonds","O<sub>2</sub> contains no bonds at all between its two oxygen atoms","Ozone has a much higher bond enthalpy than molecular oxygen","O<sub>2</sub> absorbs no electromagnetic radiation of any wavelength"],explain:"Ozone's resonance-delocalized O&ndash;O bonds are, on average, weaker than a true O=O double bond, so ozone's bonds break with lower-energy, longer-wavelength UV light (&lt;330 nm) than is needed to break O<sub>2</sub>'s stronger double bond (&lt;242 nm)."},
{ch:7,q:"Resonance structures are often compared to the idea of a 'zorse' (a cross between a zebra and a horse). What point does this analogy illustrate?",choices:["A resonance hybrid is a single, fixed intermediate structure, not a molecule that flips between two different forms over time","Resonance structures can only be drawn for animals, not molecules","A zorse has no real existence, just like a resonance hybrid","Resonance structures always involve exactly two different elements"],explain:"Just as a zorse is a genuine, fixed hybrid animal and not a creature that alternates between being a zebra and a horse, a resonance hybrid is the single true structure of a molecule or ion, not a rapid flip between the contributing Lewis structures."},
{ch:7,q:"How many valid resonance structures exist for the nitrite ion, NO<sub>2</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>?",choices:["Two, since the double bond could be placed on either of the two identical oxygen atoms","Three, one for every atom in the ion","One, since nitrite has only a single possible Lewis structure","Four, accounting for every lone pair in the ion"],explain:"Nitrite's Lewis formula has one N=O double bond and one N&ndash;O single bond; because the double bond could equally be placed on either identical oxygen atom, two equivalent resonance structures exist, giving a hybrid with two identical, intermediate N&ndash;O bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"Benzene, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>, is described as a resonance hybrid of two Lewis structures with alternating single and double C&ndash;C bonds. What is the experimental evidence that all six carbon&ndash;carbon bonds are actually identical?",choices:["X-ray diffraction shows all six C&ndash;C bond lengths in benzene are equal, at 0.139 nm, intermediate between a single bond (0.154 nm) and a double bond (0.134 nm)","Benzene has no carbon&ndash;carbon bonds detectable by any experimental method","All six C&ndash;C bonds in benzene are exactly equal to a C=C double bond's length","Benzene's carbon atoms are not arranged in a ring at all"],explain:"X-ray diffraction data confirm all six C&ndash;C bonds in benzene are equal in length (0.139 nm), a value intermediate between a typical single bond (0.154 nm) and double bond (0.134 nm), consistent with a delocalized resonance hybrid rather than fixed alternating bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"Hydrogenating benzene (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>) releases only about &minus;208 to &minus;210 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, far less than the &minus;360 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> predicted for a hypothetical molecule with three isolated, localized C=C double bonds. What does this difference represent?",choices:["The resonance (stabilization) energy of benzene, showing it is more stable (lower in energy) than the Kekul&eacute; structure would predict","Evidence that benzene does not actually contain any hydrogen atoms","Proof that benzene cannot undergo hydrogenation at all","Evidence that benzene's ring structure is unstable compared with three separate double bonds"],explain:"The difference between the predicted (&minus;360 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) and actual (&asymp;&minus;208 to &minus;210 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) enthalpy change of hydrogenation, roughly 150&ndash;152 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, is benzene's resonance energy: the extra stability gained from having its pi electrons delocalized around the whole ring rather than fixed in three separate double bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does benzene strongly resist the addition reactions typical of alkenes, favouring substitution reactions instead?",choices:["An addition reaction would disrupt the delocalized ring of pi electrons, requiring the large resonance energy to be supplied and leaving a less stable product","Benzene contains no carbon&ndash;carbon double bonds of any kind","Benzene molecules repel all other reactant molecules electrostatically","Substitution reactions release far less energy than addition reactions in every case"],explain:"Addition across benzene's ring would break up its stable, delocalized pi-electron system, costing a large amount of resonance (stabilization) energy and producing a less stable, non-aromatic product; substitution reactions instead preserve the intact aromatic ring."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does only one compound exist with the formula of 1,2-dibromobenzene, 1,3-dibromobenzene, and 1,4-dibromobenzene (three isomers in total), rather than more isomers as a simple alternating Kekul&eacute; structure might suggest?",choices:["Benzene's symmetrical, delocalized ring gives only three distinguishable relative positions for two substituents around the ring","Benzene cannot undergo any substitution reactions at all","Bromine atoms cannot bond to a benzene ring under any circumstances","Each carbon in benzene can only ever bond to one other atom"],explain:"Because benzene is a symmetrical ring with no fixed alternating single and double bonds, placing two bromine atoms on the ring gives only three distinguishable relative arrangements (adjacent, one-apart, or opposite), matching the three known isomers of dibromobenzene &mdash; evidence against a fixed Kekul&eacute; structure, which would predict additional isomers."},
{ch:7,q:"What was the main limitation of Kekul&eacute;'s 1865 model of benzene as 1,3,5-cyclohexatriene, with alternating single and double C&ndash;C bonds?",choices:["It could not explain benzene's resistance to addition reactions or its uniform (rather than alternating) bond lengths","It incorrectly predicted that benzene has more than three isomers of dibromobenzene","It could not explain why benzene has no isomers at all","It predicted that benzene contains no hydrogen atoms"],explain:"The Kekul&eacute; model correctly explained the lack of benzene isomers through its symmetry, but could not account for the experimentally observed uniform (equal) bond lengths around the ring or benzene's strong resistance to typical alkene addition reactions."},
{ch:7,q:"What is represented by the circle drawn inside the hexagon in the common skeletal representation of benzene?",choices:["The delocalized pi electrons, spread equally around the ring rather than confined to fixed double bonds","A single lone pair of electrons on one specific carbon atom","A central atom bonded to all six ring carbons","A covalent network structure extending beyond the ring"],explain:"The circle inside the benzene hexagon is a shorthand for the delocalized system of pi electrons spread evenly around the entire ring, rather than three separate, fixed double bonds alternating with single bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does a molecule such as PCl<sub>5</sub>, with a central atom from period 3, manage to have more than eight electrons around its central phosphorus atom?",choices:["Phosphorus has accessible valence d-orbitals at an energy close to its p-orbitals, allowing electron promotion and the formation of additional bonds","Phosphorus atoms are smaller than nitrogen atoms, allowing more bonds to fit around them","Chlorine atoms contribute extra valence electrons that nitrogen atoms cannot contribute","PCl<sub>5</sub> does not actually contain more than eight electrons around phosphorus"],explain:"Elements in period 3 and below have valence-shell d-orbitals at an energy close enough to their p-orbitals that electron promotion into them becomes feasible, allowing additional electron pairs to form and giving rise to an expanded octet, such as the 10 electrons around phosphorus in PCl<sub>5</sub>."},
{ch:7,q:"PCl<sub>5</sub> has five bonding electron domains and no lone pairs around its central phosphorus atom. What is its molecular geometry and bond angles?",choices:["Trigonal bipyramidal, with angles of 90&deg; and 120&deg;","Octahedral, with all angles of 90&deg;","Tetrahedral, with all angles of 109.5&deg;","Square pyramidal, with angles slightly less than 90&deg;"],explain:"With five bonding domains and no lone pairs, the domains are arranged in a trigonal bipyramidal geometry, giving two different bond angles: 90&deg; between an axial and an equatorial position, and 120&deg; between equatorial positions."},
{ch:7,q:"SF<sub>4</sub> has four bonding domains and one lone pair around its central sulfur atom. Where does the lone pair sit, and what molecular shape results?",choices:["The lone pair occupies an equatorial position in the trigonal bipyramidal arrangement, giving an unsymmetrical 'see-saw' molecular shape","The lone pair occupies an axial position, giving a perfectly symmetrical tetrahedral shape","SF<sub>4</sub> has no lone pairs on sulfur at all","The lone pair forces the molecule into a linear shape"],explain:"To minimize repulsion, the more strongly repelling lone pair occupies a less crowded equatorial position in the trigonal bipyramidal electron domain geometry; looking only at the four bonded fluorine atoms, this produces an unsymmetrical, 'see-saw' shaped molecule with bond angles slightly less than 90&deg; and 120&deg;."},
{ch:7,q:"ClF<sub>3</sub> has three bonding domains and two lone pairs around its central chlorine atom. What is its molecular shape?",choices:["T-shaped, with bond angles slightly less than 90&deg;","Trigonal planar, with bond angles of 120&deg;","Tetrahedral, with bond angles of 109.5&deg;","Linear, with a bond angle of 180&deg;"],explain:"With two lone pairs occupying two of the three equatorial positions in a trigonal bipyramidal electron domain geometry, the three bonded fluorine atoms are left in a flat T-shaped arrangement, with bond angles slightly less than 90&deg; due to the extra lone pair repulsion."},
{ch:7,q:"The triiodide ion, I<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>, has two bonding domains and three lone pairs around its central iodine atom. What is its molecular shape?",choices:["Linear, with a bond angle of 180&deg;","Bent, with a bond angle of approximately 117&deg;","T-shaped, with bond angles slightly less than 90&deg;","Trigonal planar, with bond angles of 120&deg;"],explain:"With all three lone pairs occupying the three equatorial positions of the trigonal bipyramidal electron domain geometry, the two bonded iodine atoms are left directly opposite each other in the axial positions, giving a perfectly linear molecular shape."},
{ch:7,q:"SF<sub>6</sub> has six bonding domains and no lone pairs around its central sulfur atom. What is its molecular geometry and bond angle?",choices:["Octahedral, with all bond angles equal to 90&deg;","Trigonal bipyramidal, with angles of 90&deg; and 120&deg;","Tetrahedral, with all bond angles equal to 109.5&deg;","Square planar, with all bond angles equal to 90&deg;"],explain:"With six bonding domains and no lone pairs, the domains point towards the six corners of a regular octahedron, giving an octahedral molecular shape with every F&ndash;S&ndash;F bond angle equal to 90&deg;."},
{ch:7,q:"BrF<sub>5</sub> has five bonding domains and one lone pair around its central bromine atom. What molecular shape results?",choices:["Square pyramidal, with bond angles slightly less than 90&deg;","Octahedral, with all bond angles equal to exactly 90&deg;","Trigonal bipyramidal, with angles of 90&deg; and 120&deg;","T-shaped, with bond angles slightly less than 90&deg;"],explain:"With one of the six octahedral positions occupied by a lone pair, the remaining five bonded fluorine atoms form a square pyramid (four in a square base, one at the apex); the lone pair's extra repulsion compresses the bond angles to slightly less than 90&deg;."},
{ch:7,q:"XeF<sub>4</sub> has four bonding domains and two lone pairs around its central xenon atom. Why do the two lone pairs occupy positions directly opposite each other in the octahedral electron domain geometry?",choices:["This maximizes the separation between the two highly repulsive lone pairs, leaving the four fluorine atoms in a flat, square planar arrangement with 90&deg; angles","Lone pairs always occupy adjacent positions to minimize the total volume of the molecule","XeF<sub>4</sub> actually has no lone pairs on xenon at all","The lone pairs must occupy axial positions in a trigonal bipyramidal arrangement instead"],explain:"Placing the two lone pairs on exactly opposite sides of the octahedron keeps them as far apart from each other as possible, minimizing lone pair&ndash;lone pair repulsion, and leaves the four fluorine atoms arranged in a single flat square around xenon, giving a square planar molecular shape with 90&deg; angles."},
{ch:7,q:"PCl<sub>5</sub> and SF<sub>6</sub> are both non-polar molecules. Why?",choices:["All the atoms attached to each central atom are identical, and there are no lone pairs on the central atom to break the symmetry, so their bond dipoles cancel exactly","Phosphorus and sulfur have no electronegativity of their own","PCl<sub>5</sub> and SF<sub>6</sub> contain no polar bonds at all","Both molecules have lone pairs that happen to reinforce rather than cancel the bond dipoles"],explain:"Because every bonded atom is identical and the central atom carries no lone pairs to distort the symmetry, the highly symmetrical trigonal bipyramidal (PCl<sub>5</sub>) and octahedral (SF<sub>6</sub>) arrangements cause all the bond dipoles to cancel exactly, leaving both molecules non-polar."},
{ch:7,q:"Why is XeF<sub>4</sub> a non-polar molecule, despite having two lone pairs on its central xenon atom?",choices:["The two lone pairs sit on exactly opposite sides of the xenon atom, preserving the square planar symmetry so that all four Xe&ndash;F bond dipoles still cancel","XeF<sub>4</sub> contains no polar Xe&ndash;F bonds","Lone pairs never affect the polarity of any molecule","XeF<sub>4</sub> actually has no fluorine atoms bonded directly to xenon"],explain:"Because the two lone pairs occupy exactly opposite positions in the octahedral electron domain geometry, the molecule retains a symmetrical square planar shape, so the four identical Xe&ndash;F bond dipoles still cancel exactly, leaving the molecule non-polar overall."},
{ch:7,q:"When writing the Lewis formula for PF<sub>6</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>, what is the correct total number of valence electrons to use?",choices:["48, from 5 (P) + (7 &times; 6) (six F atoms) + 1 (for the single negative charge)","42, from 5 (P) + (7 &times; 6) (six F atoms), with no adjustment for charge","41, from 5 (P) + (7 &times; 6) (six F atoms), minus 1 for the charge","35, from 5 (P) + (7 &times; 5), as if only five fluorine atoms were present"],explain:"Phosphorus contributes 5 valence electrons, six fluorine atoms contribute 7 each (42), and one additional electron is added for the ion's single negative charge, giving 5 + 42 + 1 = 48 valence electrons in total."},
{ch:7,q:"Formal charge treats every covalent bond using which simplifying assumption?",choices:["That the bond is purely covalent, with each bonding pair split exactly equally between the two bonded atoms, regardless of real electronegativity differences","That every bond is purely ionic, with both electrons assigned to the more electronegative atom","That lone pairs are shared equally between neighbouring atoms","That only atoms with an incomplete octet can be assigned a formal charge"],explain:"Formal charge deliberately ignores real differences in electronegativity, treating every bond as if it were purely covalent with the bonding pair split exactly equally (one electron credited to each atom), unlike oxidation number, which treats bonds as fully ionic."},
{ch:7,q:"Using the formula FC = V &minus; (S + L), what do S and L represent?",choices:["S is the number of bonds (shared pairs) the atom forms, and L is the number of non-bonding (lone pair) electrons it owns outright","S is the total number of valence electrons, and L is the ionic charge of the species","S is the number of lone pairs, and L is the number of bonds","S and L both represent the same quantity, counted twice for accuracy"],explain:"In the formal charge formula, V is the valence electrons of the free atom, S is the number of bonds (shared electron pairs) formed by that atom, and L is the number of non-bonding (lone pair) electrons it owns completely in the Lewis structure."},
{ch:7,q:"In one Lewis structure of SO<sub>2</sub>, sulfur has one single S&ndash;O bond, one double S=O bond, and one lone pair. What is the formal charge on sulfur in this structure?",choices:["+1, since FC = 6 &minus; (3 + 2) = +1","0, since FC = 6 &minus; (4 + 2) = 0","&minus;1, since FC = 6 &minus; (1 + 6) = &minus;1","+2, since FC = 6 &minus; (2 + 2) = +2"],explain:"Sulfur here forms 3 bonds in total (1 from the single bond + 2 from the double bond) and has 1 lone pair (2 electrons); FC = 6 &minus; (3 + 2) = +1."},
{ch:7,q:"In the alternative Lewis structure of SO<sub>2</sub> with two S=O double bonds and one lone pair on sulfur (an expanded octet of 10 electrons), what is the formal charge on sulfur?",choices:["0, since FC = 6 &minus; (4 + 2) = 0","+1, since FC = 6 &minus; (3 + 2) = +1","&minus;2, since FC = 6 &minus; (6 + 2) = &minus;2","+2, since FC = 6 &minus; (2 + 2) = +2"],explain:"With two double bonds, sulfur forms 4 bonds in total and still has 1 lone pair (2 electrons); FC = 6 &minus; (4 + 2) = 0, giving sulfur a formal charge of zero in this expanded-octet structure."},
{ch:7,q:"Between the two possible Lewis structures of SO<sub>2</sub> (one with a single and a double S&ndash;O bond, the other with two S=O double bonds and an expanded octet on sulfur), which is the preferred structure, and why?",choices:["The structure with two S=O double bonds, because every atom in it has a formal charge of zero","The structure with one single and one double bond, because sulfur keeps an unexpanded octet","Both structures are equally preferred, since they are true resonance structures of each other","Neither structure is acceptable, since SO<sub>2</sub> cannot be represented by a Lewis formula"],explain:"Calculating formal charges shows the single/double bond structure gives sulfur +1 and the two oxygens 0 and &minus;1, while the two-double-bond (expanded octet) structure gives every atom a formal charge of exactly zero; the structure with the lowest overall formal charges is preferred, so the expanded-octet structure is chosen."},
{ch:7,q:"For the preferred Lewis structure of XeO<sub>3</sub>, with three Xe=O double bonds and one lone pair on xenon, what is the formal charge on xenon?",choices:["0, since FC = 8 &minus; (6 + 2) = 0","+2, since FC = 8 &minus; (4 + 2) = +2","&minus;1, since FC = 8 &minus; (6 + 3) = &minus;1","+1, since FC = 8 &minus; (5 + 2) = +1"],explain:"With three double bonds, xenon forms 6 bonds in total and has 1 lone pair (2 electrons); FC = 8 &minus; (6 + 2) = 0, consistent with this being the preferred, lowest-formal-charge structure for XeO<sub>3</sub>."},
{ch:7,q:"Why is the Lewis structure of BF<sub>3</sub> with an incomplete octet on boron preferred over an alternative structure with a B=F double bond that completes boron's octet?",choices:["The double-bonded alternative would place a formal charge of +1 on fluorine, the most electronegative element, which is highly unfavourable, despite giving boron a complete octet","Boron cannot form double bonds to fluorine under any circumstances","The incomplete-octet structure actually has a higher overall formal charge than the double-bonded alternative","Fluorine atoms are too large to form a double bond to boron"],explain:"Converting a B&ndash;F single bond to a double bond would give boron a formal charge of &minus;1 and that fluorine atom a formal charge of +1; since fluorine is the most electronegative element, placing a positive formal charge on it is highly unfavourable, so the formal-charge-zero, incomplete-octet structure remains preferred even though it does not satisfy the octet rule."},
{ch:7,q:"Two non-equivalent Lewis structures for nitrous oxide, N<sub>2</sub>O, both give a spread of formal charges of 0, +1, and &minus;1, just distributed on different atoms. Which rule is then used to choose between them?",choices:["The preferred structure places the negative formal charge on the more electronegative atom","The structure with the larger total number of bonds is always preferred, regardless of formal charge","The preferred structure is simply chosen at random, since both are equally acceptable","The structure with the higher overall positive charge is always preferred"],explain:"When two structures tie on having the lowest possible formal charges, the preferred structure is the one that places any negative formal charge on the more electronegative atom; for N<sub>2</sub>O, this favours the structure N&equiv;N&ndash;O, which places the &minus;1 formal charge on oxygen rather than on nitrogen."},
{ch:7,q:"In any single Lewis structure, what must the sum of the formal charges on every atom equal?",choices:["The overall charge on the species (zero for a neutral molecule, or the ionic charge for an ion)","Always exactly zero, even for a charged ion","The total number of valence electrons in the species","The total number of lone pairs in the structure"],explain:"The formal charges assigned to every atom in a correctly drawn Lewis structure must always sum to the overall charge on the species, providing a useful check: zero for a neutral molecule, or matching the ionic charge for an ion."},
{ch:7,q:"How do formal charge and oxidation number differ as models for assigning charge within a covalent structure?",choices:["Formal charge treats every bond as purely covalent (electrons split equally), while oxidation number treats every bond as purely ionic (electrons assigned entirely to the more electronegative atom)","Formal charge and oxidation number are simply two different names for exactly the same calculation","Formal charge can only be calculated for ions, while oxidation number can only be calculated for neutral molecules","Oxidation number ignores electronegativity entirely, while formal charge is based entirely on it"],explain:"Formal charge and oxidation number represent opposite simplifying assumptions: formal charge assumes perfectly equal (covalent) sharing of every bonding pair, while oxidation number assumes complete (ionic) transfer of every bonding pair to the more electronegative atom; neither perfectly describes the real charge distribution, but each is useful in its own context."},
{ch:7,q:"A sigma (&sigma;) bond forms by which type of orbital overlap?",choices:["Head-on (end-on) overlap of two atomic orbitals along the bond axis, concentrating electron density directly between the two nuclei","Sideways (lateral) overlap of two parallel p orbitals, concentrating electron density above and below the bond axis","Overlap of two full, unhybridized d orbitals only","No overlap of orbitals at all; sigma bonds form purely through electrostatic attraction between ions"],explain:"A sigma bond forms by the head-on overlap of two atomic (or hybrid) orbitals directly along the bond axis, concentrating the shared electron density in the region directly between the two nuclei."},
{ch:7,q:"A pi (&pi;) bond forms by which type of orbital overlap, and under what condition?",choices:["Sideways (lateral) overlap of two parallel p orbitals, which can only occur alongside an existing sigma bond between the same two atoms","Head-on overlap of two s orbitals, occurring independently of any sigma bond","Overlap of a hybrid orbital with an s orbital, forming the first bond between any two atoms","Overlap of two orbitals on completely unrelated, non-adjacent atoms"],explain:"A pi bond forms by the sideways overlap of two parallel p orbitals, concentrating electron density in two separate regions above and below the bond axis; a pi bond can only form in addition to an already-existing sigma bond between the same two atoms, so pi bonds exist only within double or triple bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"How many sigma and how many pi bonds are present in a single covalent bond, a double covalent bond, and a triple covalent bond respectively?",choices:["Single: 1 sigma, 0 pi. Double: 1 sigma, 1 pi. Triple: 1 sigma, 2 pi.","Single: 0 sigma, 1 pi. Double: 1 sigma, 1 pi. Triple: 2 sigma, 1 pi.","Single: 1 sigma, 1 pi. Double: 2 sigma, 2 pi. Triple: 3 sigma, 3 pi.","Single: 1 sigma, 0 pi. Double: 2 sigma, 0 pi. Triple: 3 sigma, 0 pi."],explain:"Every single bond is exactly one sigma bond; a double bond is one sigma bond plus one pi bond; a triple bond is one sigma bond plus two pi bonds, with the two pi bonds lying in two different planes that both contain the bond axis."},
{ch:7,q:"Why are pi bonds generally weaker than sigma bonds between the same two atoms?",choices:["A pi bond's electron density lies away from the direct line between the nuclei, so it is less strongly attracted to both nuclei at once than a sigma bond's electron density","Pi bonds contain fewer electrons than sigma bonds","Pi bonds can only form between atoms of different elements","Sigma bonds never actually contain any shared electrons"],explain:"Because a pi bond's electron density is concentrated above and below the bond axis rather than directly between the nuclei, it is held less tightly by the simultaneous attraction of both nuclei, making it generally weaker and more reactive than a sigma bond."},
{ch:7,q:"In carbon monoxide, CO, which statement correctly describes its triple bond in terms of sigma and pi bonds?",choices:["One sigma bond and two pi bonds, exactly like any other triple bond, regardless of which atom originally donated each pair of electrons","Three sigma bonds and no pi bonds","One sigma bond and one pi bond, since CO only has a double bond","Three pi bonds and no sigma bond"],explain:"Like any triple bond, the C&equiv;O bond in carbon monoxide consists of one sigma bond and two pi bonds; the fact that one of the three shared pairs originated as a coordination bond (donated entirely by oxygen) does not change the type of orbital overlap or the sigma/pi classification of the finished bond."},
{ch:7,q:"How many sigma bonds and how many pi bonds are present in a molecule of ethyne (acetylene), C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub>, H&ndash;C&equiv;C&ndash;H?",choices:["3 sigma bonds and 2 pi bonds","2 sigma bonds and 3 pi bonds","5 sigma bonds and 1 pi bond","4 sigma bonds and 0 pi bonds"],explain:"Ethyne contains two C&ndash;H single bonds (2 sigma) and one C&equiv;C triple bond (1 sigma + 2 pi), giving a total of 3 sigma bonds and 2 pi bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"Why does carbon, with the ground-state electron configuration 1s<sup>2</sup>2s<sup>2</sup>2p<sub>x</sub><sup>1</sup>2p<sub>y</sub><sup>1</sup>, consistently form four covalent bonds instead of only two?",choices:["An electron is promoted (excited) from the 2s orbital into the empty 2p orbital, creating four singly occupied orbitals available for bonding","Carbon atoms always lose two electrons before bonding, becoming a 2+ ion first","The ground-state configuration of carbon already has four singly occupied orbitals","Carbon borrows two extra electrons from each atom it bonds to"],explain:"Excitation promotes one electron from the filled 2s orbital into the previously empty 2p orbital, giving carbon four singly occupied orbitals (one 2s-derived, three 2p) instead of only two; the small energy cost of this promotion is repaid by the greater energy released upon forming four bonds instead of two."},
{ch:7,q:"What is hybridization?",choices:["The mixing of a set of atomic orbitals within one atom to produce an equal number of new, identical hybrid orbitals with intermediate energy and shape","The transfer of electrons from one atom to another during ionic bond formation","The process by which two different atoms merge their nuclei together","The splitting of one atomic orbital into two separate, unrelated orbitals on two different atoms"],explain:"Hybridization is the mixing of atomic orbitals within a single atom (such as one s and three p orbitals) to produce a new set of equivalent hybrid orbitals, identical to one another but different from their parent orbitals, allowing stronger, more effective bond formation."},
{ch:7,q:"When carbon forms four single bonds, as in methane, CH<sub>4</sub>, what type of hybridization occurs, and what shape results?",choices:["sp<sup>3</sup> hybridization, giving four equal orbitals arranged tetrahedrally at 109.5&deg;","sp<sup>2</sup> hybridization, giving three equal orbitals arranged at 120&deg;","sp hybridization, giving two equal orbitals arranged linearly at 180&deg;","No hybridization occurs; methane's bonds form directly from unhybridized s and p orbitals"],explain:"Mixing one s orbital with all three p orbitals produces four equal sp<sup>3</sup> hybrid orbitals, oriented towards the four corners of a tetrahedron at 109.5&deg; to each other, matching methane's four identical, tetrahedrally arranged C&ndash;H bonds."},
{ch:7,q:"When carbon forms a double bond, as in ethene, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>, what type of hybridization occurs on each carbon atom, and how many unhybridized p orbitals remain?",choices:["sp<sup>2</sup> hybridization, leaving one unhybridized p orbital perpendicular to the plane of the three hybrid orbitals","sp<sup>3</sup> hybridization, leaving no unhybridized p orbitals at all","sp hybridization, leaving two unhybridized p orbitals","sp<sup>2</sup> hybridization, leaving three unhybridized p orbitals"],explain:"Mixing one s orbital with only two of the three p orbitals produces three equal sp<sup>2</sup> hybrid orbitals (trigonal planar, 120&deg;), leaving one p orbital completely unhybridized, standing perpendicular to the plane; this leftover p orbital goes on to form the pi bond of the C=C double bond."},
{ch:7,q:"In ethene, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>, how does the carbon&ndash;carbon double bond form from the sp<sup>2</sup> hybrid orbitals and remaining p orbitals on each carbon?",choices:["One sp<sup>2</sup> orbital from each carbon overlaps end-on to form a sigma bond, and the one remaining unhybridized p orbital on each carbon overlaps sideways to form a pi bond","Both the sigma and pi bonds form entirely from sp<sup>2</sup> hybrid orbitals overlapping end-on","The double bond forms from two separate sigma bonds, with no pi bond involved","All three sp<sup>2</sup> orbitals on each carbon combine together to form the double bond"],explain:"The sigma component of the C=C double bond comes from one sp<sup>2</sup> hybrid orbital on each carbon overlapping end-on; the pi component comes from the one unhybridized p orbital on each carbon, oriented perpendicular to the sp<sup>2</sup> plane, overlapping sideways with its counterpart on the other carbon."},
{ch:7,q:"When carbon forms a triple bond, as in ethyne, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub>, what type of hybridization occurs on each carbon atom, and how many unhybridized p orbitals remain?",choices:["sp hybridization, leaving two unhybridized p orbitals, mutually perpendicular to each other and to the sp orbitals","sp<sup>3</sup> hybridization, leaving no unhybridized p orbitals","sp<sup>2</sup> hybridization, leaving one unhybridized p orbital","sp hybridization, leaving three unhybridized p orbitals"],explain:"Mixing one s orbital with only one of the three p orbitals produces two equal sp hybrid orbitals (linear, 180&deg;), leaving two p orbitals completely unhybridized and mutually perpendicular; these two leftover p orbitals go on to form the two pi bonds of the C&equiv;C triple bond."},
{ch:7,q:"Can a lone (non-bonding) pair of electrons occupy a hybrid orbital, as seen in ammonia and water?",choices:["Yes; in NH<sub>3</sub> one of nitrogen's four sp<sup>3</sup> orbitals holds the lone pair, and in H<sub>2</sub>O two of oxygen's four sp<sup>3</sup> orbitals each hold a lone pair","No; lone pairs can only ever occupy completely unhybridized s or p orbitals","No; hybridization is only possible for atoms with no lone pairs at all","Yes, but only if the lone pair belongs to a hydrogen atom"],explain:"Hybrid orbitals are not reserved only for bonding electrons; in ammonia, nitrogen is sp<sup>3</sup> hybridized with one of its four sp<sup>3</sup> orbitals holding the lone pair, and in water, oxygen is sp<sup>3</sup> hybridized with two of its four sp<sup>3</sup> orbitals each holding a lone pair."},
{ch:7,q:"What is the correct relationship linking the number of electron domains, the electron domain geometry, and the hybridization of a central atom?",choices:["2 domains &harr; linear &harr; sp; 3 domains &harr; trigonal planar &harr; sp<sup>2</sup>; 4 domains &harr; tetrahedral &harr; sp<sup>3</sup>","2 domains &harr; linear &harr; sp<sup>3</sup>; 3 domains &harr; trigonal planar &harr; sp; 4 domains &harr; tetrahedral &harr; sp<sup>2</sup>","Hybridization depends only on molecular mass, not on the number of electron domains","2 domains &harr; tetrahedral &harr; sp<sup>2</sup>; 3 domains &harr; linear &harr; sp; 4 domains &harr; trigonal planar &harr; sp<sup>3</sup>"],explain:"The number of electron domains around a central atom determines both the electron domain geometry (via VSEPR) and the hybridization required to produce that geometry: 2 domains give a linear arrangement from sp hybridization, 3 domains give trigonal planar from sp<sup>2</sup>, and 4 domains give tetrahedral from sp<sup>3</sup>."},
{ch:7,q:"Although NH<sub>3</sub> is trigonal pyramidal and H<sub>2</sub>O is bent (not tetrahedral) in their final molecular shapes, both central atoms are still described as sp<sup>3</sup> hybridized. Why?",choices:["Hybridization follows the total electron domain geometry (always tetrahedral for 4 domains), regardless of how many of those domains are lone pairs rather than bonded atoms","NH<sub>3</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O actually have different numbers of electron domains from each other","Hybridization is determined only by molecular mass, not by electron domain count","sp<sup>3</sup> hybridization can only ever apply to carbon atoms"],explain:"Hybridization depends on the total number of electron domains (bonding and non-bonding together), which is four for both NH<sub>3</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O, giving sp<sup>3</sup> hybridization in both cases; the different final molecular shapes (trigonal pyramidal and bent) arise only because lone pairs are excluded when naming the <em>molecular</em> geometry, not the hybridization."},
{ch:7,q:"In urea, H<sub>2</sub>N&ndash;C(=O)&ndash;NH<sub>2</sub>, what is the hybridization of the central carbon atom, and what are its approximate bond angles?",choices:["sp<sup>2</sup>, with bond angles of approximately 120&deg;, since carbon has three electron domains (one C=O double bond and two C&ndash;N single bonds)","sp<sup>3</sup>, with bond angles of approximately 109.5&deg;","sp, with bond angles of 180&deg;","sp<sup>3</sup>, with bond angles of approximately 107&deg;, as if carbon had a lone pair"],explain:"Carbon in urea has three electron domains around it (one C=O double bond, two C&ndash;N single bonds, no lone pairs), giving a trigonal planar electron domain geometry, sp<sup>2</sup> hybridization, and bond angles of approximately 120&deg;."},
{ch:7,q:"In urea, H<sub>2</sub>N&ndash;C(=O)&ndash;NH<sub>2</sub>, what is the hybridization of each nitrogen atom?",choices:["sp<sup>3</sup>, since each nitrogen has four electron domains (two N&ndash;H bonds, one N&ndash;C bond, and one lone pair)","sp<sup>2</sup>, since each nitrogen has only three electron domains","sp, since each nitrogen has only two electron domains","Nitrogen atoms cannot be hybridized under any circumstances"],explain:"Each nitrogen in urea is bonded to two hydrogens and the central carbon, and carries one lone pair, giving four electron domains in total; this tetrahedral electron domain geometry corresponds to sp<sup>3</sup> hybridization on each nitrogen."},
{ch:7,q:"Each carbon atom in benzene is sp<sup>2</sup> hybridized. What happens to the one unhybridized p orbital remaining on each carbon?",choices:["Instead of pairing into three separate, localized pi bonds, all six p orbitals overlap sideways with both neighbours simultaneously, spreading six pi electrons evenly around the whole ring","Each unhybridized p orbital simply remains empty and takes no further part in bonding","The unhybridized p orbitals combine to form additional sigma bonds within the ring","The unhybridized p orbitals leave the carbon atoms entirely once the sigma framework has formed"],explain:"Rather than forming three fixed, alternating pi bonds as a simple Kekul&eacute; structure would suggest, the six unhybridized p orbitals (one per carbon, all perpendicular to the ring) overlap sideways with both ring neighbours at once, spreading the pi electrons evenly around the entire ring as a delocalized system, pictured as two donut-shaped electron clouds above and below the ring plane."},
{ch:7,q:"Why is hybridization, despite having a genuine basis in quantum mechanical wave functions, often taught and visualized using a simpler 'mixing' model instead?",choices:["The full mathematical treatment is complex, so the simpler model is used because it successfully explains and predicts real molecular geometries that match experimental observation","Quantum mechanics has no real connection to hybridization at all","The simpler model is used only because no mathematical description of hybridization actually exists","Hybridization is purely a historical idea with no modern scientific basis"],explain:"Hybridization genuinely arises from combining atomic wave functions in quantum mechanics, but because this mathematics is complex, the simpler 'orbital mixing' model is commonly used instead; this simplified model is validated by how successfully it explains and predicts real molecular geometries, the same standard used to judge the VSEPR model."}
]
